  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing shower and thunderstorm chances this\nweekend into next week.\n\nExpecting another dry summer day as weak high pressure moves over \nthe region. Temps will be slightly warmer in the upper 80s to low \n90s, with light northwest winds through this evening. Southerly flow \nreturns tonight into this weekend as the high pressure moves \noffshore. in its wake a potent upper level trough moves east from \nthe Great Lakes toward the Ohio River Valley. The upper level trough \nthen pinches off into a cut low near Ohio late Sunday night. The \nassociated cold front is progged to slowly track across the area \nduring the second half of the weekend into early next week. \n\nMoisture return ahead of this system begins Saturday where scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast. Convection looks \nto be focused mainly west of the Blue Ridge on Saturday, then \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms are most likely Sunday into \nSunday night. Shear and instability values look to remain low due to \nweak winds aloft and widespread cloud cover. Locally heavy rainfall \nis possible Sunday night, but the exact placement and intensity will \ndepend on where the cutoff low and jet streak aloft are located. A \nfew ensemble members show the possibility of rain amounts upwards of \n2-3\" if favorable dynamics come together. \n\nOn Monday, the cutoff surface low pressure system near the Ohio \nRiver Valley continues to fill in and increase in pressure. Although \nensembles tend to weaken the low pressure system Monday, widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms chances continue throughout the day with \nabundant cloud cover. \n\nAs the upper level trough associated with Monday's weather leaves \nour region Tuesday, conditions remain unstable with an abnormally \nmoist airmass remaining over our region through much of next week. \nWith peak heating, diurnal thunderstorms cannot be ruled out next \nweek with chances increasing into the work week.",
